Tell the engine which game it is playing.

lichess-bot runs `concurrency: 2` and spawns one engine process per game.
Every one of them writes to the same `logs/engine.jsonl`, and nothing in a
record said which game it came from -- so a reader gets two narrations spliced
together with no way to unpick them. The dashboard's evaluation chart plotted
both games at once and its search panel flipped between two boards; it was
reported as "the graph says one side is winning when it is not".

The dashboard can infer the answer -- the lichess stream knows which positions
this game has been in, and the engine only searches on its own turn -- but
inference is inference, and in the opening the two games can be in the same
position for a few plies.

`extra_game_handlers.game_specific_options` is upstream's own hook for exactly
this: it is handed the game and returns UCI options to configure the engine
with at spawn. So pass the game id, and the engine stamps it on every record it
writes (`sumofish/telemetry.py`). No inference left.

The engine declares `option name GameId type string`, so this is safe in either
order: an engine without the option would make `engine.configure()` raise and
close, so deploy the engine side first. It does not affect play in any way --
nothing reads the value except the narration.

Applied by patches/apply.sh, which is idempotent. Re-run after pulling
lichess-bot.

--- a/extra_game_handlers.py
+++ b/extra_game_handlers.py
@@
 def game_specific_options(game: model.Game) -> OPTIONS_TYPE:
     """
     Return a dictionary of engine options based on game aspects.

     By default, an empty dict is returned so that the options in the configuration file are used.
     """
-    return {}
+    # PATCHED (see patches/0003-engine-game-id.patch): stamp the game id on the
+    # engine's telemetry, so two concurrent games can be told apart in one log.
+    return {"GameId": game.id}
